tsheet alpha1
============

Fortran numbers
- 1.5D+03, 1.d2, 2.0Q-5 and 1.234567-100 (E-less, three-digit exponent) are
  numbers: in cells, formulas (=1.5D3*2), files, paste, sorting, statistics,
  graphs and XLSX/ODS export. Blanks around a number no longer make it text.
- Format > Numbers > Fortran: 1.0D+03. Decimal places sets the mantissa digits.
- Opening a delimited file, pasting or typing sets that format on every cell
  written in Fortran form, keeping its mantissa digits (1.d2 -> 1.D+02).
- Plain (or no number format) shows Fortran text as an ordinary number:
  1.5D+03 as 1500, 6.02D+23 as 6.02e+23. Switching into or out of Fortran
  starts the digits afresh, so Fortran digits never become decimal places.
- Graph: x format and y format offer a list, Fortran style first
  (%.1tD%+03T), plus Custom... and Default. Right-click > Graph > Fortran
  labels X / Y toggle them.
- A number too wide for its column is shown with fewer digits, its exponent
  kept, or as #### when it cannot fit. Digits are never cut off.

Delimiters
- Delimited files have their delimiter detected (tab, semicolon, pipe, comma,
  spaces); "1,5;2,7" is read as semicolon data with decimal commas.
- Opening one asks for the delimiter, the detected one highlighted, with a
  preview of the first line. File > Import > "Ask for delimiter on open"
  toggles the question; "Delimiter: auto-detect" is the new default.
- Ctrl+S writes a CSV back with the delimiter it was read with.

Paste
- Edit > Paste > Delimited into rows / into columns: clipboard text split on its
  detected delimiter, each line laid out as a row or as a column.
- Ctrl+V splits multi-line text that is plainly a table (every line splits the
  same way); a single sentence with a comma stays one cell.

Graphs
- Non-rectangular selections: Ctrl-click areas, L shapes, single columns.
- ~/.config/tsheet/plot holds the gnuplot commands, one per line; edited by
  hand or by Graph options, reread before every graph. Placeholders @SERIES,
  @DATA, @N, @XNAME fill in the plot line.

Export and import
- Export: XLSX and ODS are in the Export menu.
- File > Export options: delimiter (comma, semicolon, tab, pipe), CRLF line
  endings, and "Selection only", which saves just the selected block.
- File > Import options: delimiter used when opening CSV files.
- Writers stay libc-only: a small stored-zip writer builds XLSX and ODS.

Clipboard
- Tab-separated paste trims spaces around each field; blank fields become
  empty cells. HTML paste already collapsed whitespace.

Menus
- Hovering over a title in the menu bar highlights it, and opens its pop-up
  when there is one (Graph opens its dialog on click only).
- Graph menu is a pop-up list: draw, view, and all graph options.
- Graph > View in gnuplot window opens the graph in gnuplot's own window
  (gnuplot -persist), instead of rendering a PDF.

Program
- Program is named tsheet (make installs tsheet). --version prints it.

Copy and paste
- With-formatting copies put the displayed text on the clipboard as well as the
  HTML, so a plain-text paste shows what the cells show.
- HTML copies keep bold, highlight and alignment; LaTeX copies do too
  (\\textbf, \\colorbox) and escape the prefix and suffix.
- Pasting from another book (HTML or text) keeps the formats it can read: bold
  weight, a background that is not white (highlight), text alignment, and numbers
  with a prefix or suffix. "$1,234.50" becomes 1234.50 with a $ prefix, comma
  grouping and two decimals; "12 kg" becomes 12 with the suffix " kg"; "25%" is
  25 with the suffix "%"; "(1,234)" is -1234 (shown with a minus sign). Text that
  only looks numeric ("Room 12", "3 apples 4") stays text.
- Pasted cells take the source's format, and plain text clears it, as a paste
  from another book does.

Convert
- Convert menu (top bar): To unit... converts the selection to a unit; type
  "ft", or "m ft" when the plain numbers are metres. Then 13 categories of
  common scientific conversions (70 pairs): length, mass, time, volume, area,
  pressure, energy, power, force, speed, temperature, angle, frequency. Each
  item shows its factor in the status bar.
- A cell with a unit as its suffix converts from that unit; a plain number takes
  the item's "from" unit. A formula stays a formula, =(x)*a+b, unless it reads a
  cell of the selection (it then follows that cell). Quantities of another kind
  are left alone. Temperatures use their offsets (°C, °F, K, °R).
- Limits: a formula's reference is not rewritten; a range that spans the selection
  with both ends outside it counts as outside.

Spreadsheet files
- Open reads .xlsx and .ods (first sheet). Shared strings, numbers, booleans,
  formulas (kept as formulas), merged cells, repeated and spanned cells, and
  text spacing are read. Zip and deflate are built in (read.c), no libraries.
- XLSX and ODS save now record merged blocks (mergeCells, spans), and write only
  the top-left cell's value into a merged block.
- Limits: formulas are read as written (references converted); charts, styles,
  column widths and other sheets are not read; a file longer than 10000 rows is
  cut at that row.

Numbers
- Format > Prefix... and Suffix... set text shown before and after numbers. Both
  take UTF-8 (€, kg, %) or LaTeX (\\euro, \\text{kg}, m^{2}, H_{2}O); LaTeX is
  shown as UTF-8. Text cells are not changed. Empty clears.
- Format > Numbers: Plain, Comma 1,000 (1,234.5), Space 1 000, Scientific
  1*10^3 (1.234*10^3, 0.5*10^-3), Engineering 1 k (1.234 k, k to E; under 1000
  shown plain). These apply to whole and decimal numbers; decimal places still
  apply with grouping.
- A suffix after an engineering number gets its own word: 1.234 k kg.

Format, warnings and colours
- Format is a hover pop-up. An option shows * when the whole selection has it:
  bold, highlight, alignment, date format and decimal places.
  Choosing an option applies it to the whole selection. If only some cells have
  it, choosing it applies it to all of them; if all have it, it is removed. This
  is the rule for every format option: bold, highlight, alignment, date format,
  decimal places and integer styles.
  Bold, highlight and highlighting stay visible while a block is selected.
  A range formats every cell in it, including empty cells past the data. A whole
  column or row formats up to the last used row or column only.
- Bold, highlight, date format and decimal places toggle across the selection:
  removed when every cell has them, added to every cell otherwise. Date and
  decimal places ask for a format when they are added.
- Highlighted cells have dark text on the yellow.
- Hovering a menu bar title uses the same colours as an open menu, Graph included.
- Closing or quitting a sheet that is not saved or exported warns in red. Saving
  or exporting (any format, or a selection) clears the warning. Undo, redo and
  resizing a column or row count as changes.

Import, export and menus
- File > Import... and File > Export... open dialogs like the Graph options:
  each row has a description, the current settings show as [*], and choosing a
  delimiter or toggle updates the list in place.
- Graph in the menu bar opens the all-options dialog directly. The right-click
  Graph menu keeps its quick items.
- Space delimited text, for import and export (Delimiter: space). On import a
  run of spaces is one separator and blank ends are ignored; quoted fields keep
  their spaces. On export a blank cell is written as "" so columns line up.
- Opening a file with no known extension uses the import delimiter.
- The File menu keeps New, Open, Find, Save, Import..., Export..., Close and Quit.

Selection
- Ctrl+click adds an area, as in Excel; drag with Ctrl to grow the new one.
  Ctrl+click on a selected cell unselects it, even inside a larger area: the
  area is split around it. Ctrl+click on a lit column or row header unselects
  that column or row. The corner where the headers meet selects the used range.
  At least one cell always stays selected.
  Plain clicks, arrows and Esc drop the areas; Shift+click extends the active one.
- Clear, format and the status bar (count, sum, average) work on every area;
  a cell in two areas counts once. Copy, cut, paste, fill, sort, merge, graph
  and selection-only export need one area and say so.
- Column and row headers: Ctrl+click adds a whole column or row.
- Clearing skips empty cells: clearing a 7-million-cell extent is about 13
  times faster and records about 270 times fewer undo entries.

Earlier
- Sort ascending/descending on the selection, Go to cell (Ctrl+G).
